{"name":"Tranquil Eve","hex":"#ece7f2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tranquil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Free from emotional or mental disturbance.","Calm; without motion or sound."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tranquil"},{"term":"eve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Even."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The day or night before, usually used for holidays, such as Christmas Eve.","Evening, night."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tranquillity","blocks":[{"text":"Tranquillity is the quality or state of being tranquil; that is, calm, serene, and worry-free. The word tranquillity appears in numerous texts ranging from the religious writings of Buddhism—where the term passaddhi refers to tranquillity of the body, thoughts, and consciousness on the path to enlightenment—to an assortment of policy and planning guidance documents, where interpretation of the word is typically linked to engagement with the natural environment. It is also famously used in the Preamble to the United States Constitution, which describes one of the purposes for which the document was establishing the government as to \"insure domestic Tranquility\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/John-constable-the-hay-wain.jpg/330px-John-constable-the-hay-wain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tranquillity"},{"title":"Eve","blocks":[{"text":"Eve is a figure from the Book of Genesis in the Old Testament. According to the origin story of the Abrahamic religions, she was the first woman to be created by God. Eve is known also as Adam's wife."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eve"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Sunrise_In_Lagos%2C_Nigeria_Feb_2023.jpg/500px-Sunrise_In_Lagos%2C_Nigeria_Fe